steps.body:
`class Truck extends Vehicle` makes `Truck` a subclass of `Vehicle`. A truck *is a* vehicle: every `Truck` object contains all of `Vehicle`'s fields, can call its public and protected methods, and adds its own. Shared code lives once in the parent.

```java
class Truck extends Vehicle {
    private int capacity;

    public Truck(String name, int wheels, int capacity) {
        super(name, wheels);      // run Vehicle's constructor first
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }
}
```

Constructors are not inherited, so each class writes its own. `super(...)` calls the parent constructor and must be the first line. If you leave it out, Java inserts `super()` with no arguments, which is a compile error when the parent has no constructor without parameters.

Mark parent fields `protected` when subclasses need them by name. A `private` field still exists inside every `Truck`, but `Truck`'s code cannot touch it directly and must use a getter. (In a one-file program `protected` members are also visible to other classes in the file, since they share a package.)

A class extends exactly one parent: `class Truck extends Vehicle, Machine` is a compile error. Java allows single inheritance only, though a parent can have many subclasses.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle car = new Vehicle("Sedan", 4);
        Truck truck = new Truck("Hauler", 6, 12);
        System.out.println(car.describe());
        System.out.println(truck.describe());
        System.out.println(truck.loadInfo());
        System.out.println("Name: " + truck.getName());
    }
}

class Vehicle {
    protected String name;
    protected int wheels;

    public Vehicle(String name, int wheels) {
        this.name = name;
        this.wheels = wheels;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public String describe() {
        return name + " has " + wheels + " wheels";
    }
}

class Truck extends Vehicle {
    private int capacity;

    public Truck(String name, int wheels, int capacity) {
        super(name, wheels);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public String loadInfo() {
        return name + " carries " + capacity + " tons";
    }
}

