steps.body:
A subclass can replace an inherited method by declaring one with the same name and parameter types and the same return type (or a subclass of it, for object types). Calls on a `Truck` then run the `Truck` version. Always mark it with `@Override`:

```java
@Override
public String describe() {
    return super.describe() + " and carries " + capacity + " tons";
}
```

`@Override` makes the compiler check that the parent really has a method with this exact signature. Without it, a typo like `descibe()` or an extra parameter quietly creates a brand new method, and the parent version keeps running. With it, the mistake is a compile error.

`super.describe()` runs the parent's version, so the child adds to it instead of copying its code. Calling plain `describe()` there would run the `Truck` version again, which calls itself until the program crashes with a `StackOverflowError`.

Methods you do not override are inherited unchanged. An override cannot be less visible than the original: overriding a `public` method without `public` is a compile error. That is why every `toString()` override is `public`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle car = new Vehicle("Sedan", 4);
        Truck truck = new Truck("Hauler", 6, 12);
        System.out.println(car.describe());
        System.out.println(truck.describe());
        System.out.println(truck.start());
    }
}

class Vehicle {
    protected String name;
    protected int wheels;

    public Vehicle(String name, int wheels) {
        this.name = name;
        this.wheels = wheels;
    }

    public String start() {
        return name + " starts";
    }

    public String describe() {
        return name + " has " + wheels + " wheels";
    }
}

class Truck extends Vehicle {
    private int capacity;

    public Truck(String name, int wheels, int capacity) {
        super(name, wheels);
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return super.describe() + " and carries " + capacity + " tons";
    }
}

