steps.body:
A variable of the parent type can hold any subclass object, because a `VipTicket` *is a* `Ticket`. That lets one list hold every kind:

```java
Ticket t = new VipTicket("Ben", 12.50);
List<Ticket> tickets = new ArrayList<>();
tickets.add(new ChildTicket("Cai", 12.50));
```

When you call `t.price()`, Java looks at the actual object, not the variable's type, and runs the most specific override. This is dynamic dispatch. Writing code against the parent type so that it works for every subclass is called **polymorphism**. One loop over `List<Ticket>` prices every kind of ticket correctly, with no `if` per type.

The variable's type decides what you are allowed to call. If only `VipTicket` has a `lounge()` method, then `t.lounge()` is a compile error even though the object is a VIP ticket.

A class with no `extends` extends `Object`, so every class sits under `Object` and `Object any = t;` compiles. `println(any)` calls `toString()`, which dispatches too. A `toString()` that calls `price()` gets the subclass price.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Ticket> tickets = new ArrayList<>();
        tickets.add(new Ticket("Ana", 12.50));
        tickets.add(new VipTicket("Ben", 12.50));
        tickets.add(new ChildTicket("Cai", 12.50));

        double total = 0;
        for (Ticket t : tickets) {
            double price = t.price();
            System.out.printf("%s: %.2f%n", t.getHolder(), price);
            total += price;
        }
        System.out.printf("Total: %.2f%n", total);

        Object any = tickets.get(1);
        System.out.println(any);
    }
}

class Ticket {
    protected String holder;
    protected double basePrice;

    public Ticket(String holder, double basePrice) {
        this.holder = holder;
        this.basePrice = basePrice;
    }

    public String getHolder() {
        return holder;
    }

    public double price() {
        return basePrice;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return holder + " pays " + String.format("%.2f", price());
    }
}

class VipTicket extends Ticket {
    public VipTicket(String holder, double basePrice) {
        super(holder, basePrice);
    }

    @Override
    public double price() {
        return super.price() * 2 + 5;
    }
}

class ChildTicket extends Ticket {
    public ChildTicket(String holder, double basePrice) {
        super(holder, basePrice);
    }

    @Override
    public double price() {
        return super.price() / 2;
    }
}

